WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.909
Peter Desberg, co-author of Now That’s Funny:
Should we be choreographing our leg thing

00:00:02.909 --> 00:00:03.909
here?

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:04.909
Jeffrey Davis, co-author of Now That’s Funny:
Well, I like to do this.

00:00:04.909 --> 00:00:08.470
Film Courage: Well, you know…you are each
your own person…even though you share sort

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:11.150
of one mind for the writing, you are each
your own person.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:12.150
Peter: We do?

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.150
Jeffrey: Who says we share the same mind?

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:14.150
Peter: Whose mind are we sharing?

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.370
Jeffrey: Neither of ours.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:19.790
Peter: If I were sharing his mind, you’d
see the perforations.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:23.910
Film Courage: Well, you are your own people
for the sake of this interview so you can

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.100
each do whatever you want.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.050
We’ll start with you then Jeffrey.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:31.029
How did you break into television?

00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:36.620
Jeffrey: Well…let’s see…a lot of this
is going to get cut, right?

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:37.620
Peter: Edited.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:38.620
Jeffrey: Edited?

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:41.780
Film Courage: Um…I think as long as there
are no names that are implicated, I think

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:42.780
everything will be okay.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:43.780
Jeffrey: Oh, okay.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:51.970
Well the way I broke into TV is that my father,
my uncle, my first stepmother, my stepfather

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:54.079
were all in the industry.

00:00:54.079 --> 00:01:01.170
So I grew up around it and that meant I knew
pretty much knew everybody and not a single

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:04.850
one of them gave me a job (at first).

00:01:04.850 --> 00:01:10.479
Peter laughs at my jokes [Jeffrey nudges Peter].

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:16.049
My first job was delivering call sheets at
Paramount, which of course we don’t do anymore

00:01:16.049 --> 00:01:20.490
because everything is sent by email.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:26.310
Then one of my dad’s writing partners was
a guy named Al Levitt (his real name was Al

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.529
Levitt) he was blacklisted (he and his wife
were blacklisted) and they wrote under the

00:01:30.529 --> 00:01:32.859
name of Tom and Helen August.

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:37.959
And many years later when the blacklist was
over and my dad was greylisted which meant

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:43.260
you were associated with…yeah I don’t
think I’ve told this story before…if you

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:50.369
were greylisted you were at some point going
to be called before The Committee.

00:01:50.369 --> 00:01:55.259
But his friends (The Levitt’s) after the
blacklist was over, they were working on an

00:01:55.259 --> 00:02:00.350
(boy this is a long story) they were working
on an Aaron Spelling show.

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:06.859
It was the period when Love Boat was on (which
I later worked on) and Fantasy Island and

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:12.099
they wanted to do a show called…I think
Aaron had sold a show called Paradise Hotel.

00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:20.349
And in those days they would give you the
pilot script and 6 episodes and then they

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:22.190
would decide if they were going to shoot it.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:23.840
They didn’t always shoot the pilot.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:26.180
So they gave me a script.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.510
I had never written television before.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:35.959
I think I tried to write a screenplay in college
(not grad school) because I was going to be

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:38.560
the Great American Novelist.

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:45.510
And I got paid for it, I got in the Writer’s
Guild and then ABC decided not to make the

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:46.510
show.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:47.879
And that was my first job.

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:52.720
And then I met my writing partner (my long-term
writing partner) who I had a lot in common

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.219
with because her great uncles and her grandfather
were The Fleischer Brothers.

00:02:58.219 --> 00:03:04.280
So Popeye, Betty Boop, Out of the Inkwell
and we worked together 12 years and that’s

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:11.849
the partnership that Hollywood likes the best
is male-female partners.

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:16.640
Next they like partners because they are getting
two minds for the price of one.

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.140
Film Courage: And so are we today.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.780
Jeffrey: So that’s sort of my background,
but I was around it all my life…and boy

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:25.819
that was long-winded even by my standards.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:28.459
You can cut around it I’m sure.

00:03:28.459 --> 00:03:33.030
Film Courage: Peter for you…joke writer…when
did you begin?

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:36.550
Or maybe that wasn’t the first thing you
did?

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:37.590
Jeffrey: Yesterday.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:46.739
Peter: Well, surprisingly I came in from the
academic side and started studying the psychology

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:56.719
of humor and started doing research in it
and then I took some courses in comedy writing,

00:03:56.719 --> 00:04:00.299
stand up and got more and more immersed.

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:05.930
And next thing you know, I was writing joke
books.

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:09.900
We wrote a few kid joke books, they sold pretty
well.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:18.919
And then started writing adult joke books
(fun stuff)…not as long or as good as Jeffrey’s

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:19.919
story.

00:04:19.919 --> 00:04:21.539
Jeffrey: Certainly not as long.

00:04:21.539 --> 00:04:29.419
Film Courage: You also balance your career
moonlighting as a therapist or a psychologist?

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:39.669
Peter: Well, I started out as a research psychologist,
later switched over to (or added) clinical

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:45.400
psychology to where…actually here’s another
in a series of boring stories since Jeffrey

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.419
started the tradition.

00:04:49.419 --> 00:04:55.229
I developed a course called Instructional
Humor which was a course for public speakers

00:04:55.229 --> 00:05:01.800
for how to get their point across and in doing
that I watched how nervous students would

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.250
get when they had to get up and deliver anything
is how I got interested in stage fright.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:10.060
Film Courage: Okay interesting.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:15.870
Is there room for jokes when you are doing
clinical research amongst the colleagues?

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:21.440
Or that’s where you got your material from?

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:28.620
Peter: Interestingly, stories help people
remember things.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.810
When I was a professor anything I wanted students
to remember I had to come up with a clever

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:38.800
or funny example or a clever or funny story
to get them to remember the concept.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:41.270
Otherwise…[imitates a bird flying away].

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.410
It’s a survival skill.

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:48.660
The way the mind works, it remembers stories
better than facts.

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:54.819
Jeffrey: That explains so much about history
and science for me.

00:05:54.819 --> 00:05:55.819
Not really.

00:05:55.819 --> 00:05:56.819
Sorry.

00:05:56.819 --> 00:05:58.259
Film Courage: Have either of you tried stand-up?

00:05:58.259 --> 00:06:01.139
I think I remember you saying that you had?

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:02.860
Jeffrey: Yes.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:11.080
My first writing partner Julie Fleischer had
been in…I don’t know…you guys came here

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:19.669
too late but the Kentucky Fried Theater was
sort of the thing of the moment and they did

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:24.949
improv shows which were scripted actually
I found out later and then they would do some

00:06:24.949 --> 00:06:29.220
improv with the audience.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:35.259
And their parents went on and made AIRPLANE
later (Zucker, Abraham and Zucker).

00:06:35.259 --> 00:06:36.300
They were cousins and brothers.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.889
Peter: The Zucker Brothers.

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:45.110
Jeffrey: And she went to work for them and
had long been an actress when I met her.

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:52.140
She had been an actress and a stand-up and
one night she did convince me to go to The

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:56.280
Comedy Store and I…it was really bad.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.330
Film Courage: It was a Jan Brady moment?

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.500
Jeffrey: People were asking for their money
back.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:05.250
It doesn’t get worse than that.

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:10.050
I wanted to be an actor when I was in high
school.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.699
I don’t think I’ve told you this story?

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:20.139
I think I was doing Inherit The Wind which
you know is the two main characters are based

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:27.389
on William Jennings Bryan and Clarence Darrow
and the both have to remember these long speeches

00:07:27.389 --> 00:07:32.351
and I had to memorize the speech from The
Bible and I went out halfway through it and

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:38.580
the drama teacher said “Maybe not?”

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:45.909
So I think I became a writer…writers are
very often people who fail at everything else

00:07:45.909 --> 00:07:48.250
so that’s been my experience, you know.

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.210
I did try a lot of other things.

00:07:51.210 --> 00:07:56.400
Film Courage: Or maybe they’re comfortable
in their whole element though?

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.810
That’s a whole other topic.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:06.530
Jeffrey: Despite the fact that I love to be
around people, I love being alone in a room

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:11.289
and I think you have to have that, too.

00:08:11.289 --> 00:08:14.540
Peter is much more sociable than I am.

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:15.810
He’s good in crowds.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:22.430
Peter: But I remember taking a stand-up class
and the first day the instructor got up and

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:25.110
said “Comedy is a really aggressive art
form.

00:08:25.110 --> 00:08:28.360
And if you can’t take it, get out!”

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:33.100
Which kind of stuck with me and the couple
times I got up there and I tried it I said

00:08:33.100 --> 00:08:36.140
“Hmmm, harder than it look on TV.”

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:45.690
But my wife plays the accordion and we play
in a group together and I get to write the

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:50.820
songs in genre with accordion plays in an
attempt to make them funny.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.840
And I get to do all the patter between there
which satisfies my need to do that.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:01.670
Film Courage: Okay, but again you feel comfortable
behind a typewriter or a computer is it?

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:04.110
Peter: As we say in Hollywood, I go both ways.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:07.980
Jeffrey: He’s been waiting all week to say
that.

00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.850
Film Courage: Yes…I’m not sure how to
answer that?

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:13.140
Jeffrey: Don’t even try.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:14.850
Take some advice.

